{"title":"2007 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 24","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For each <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Integer\" title=\"Integer\">integer</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/2/65262bf247c4a3c6114a8223d249dc9702619d3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&gt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" />, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/6/2663041fd2b9fbd85c85ad832f67c7a743a09c52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" /> be the number of solutions to the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Equation\" title=\"Equation\">equation</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/8/fb833752680991313822eb3d8032c721281c6846.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin{x}=\\sin{(nx)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"119\" height=\"18\" /> on the interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/e/ece27ba559bd93cdf2416aa0f88fc8d7d85e5791.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[0,\\pi]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" />. What is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/e/f3ece9235b34a4c79069ed978a0ef577a213f53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{n=2}^{2007} F(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"67\" height=\"50\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/2/f4275242690c4a978ac6919ba494f76994f55e4d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathrm{(A)}\\ 2014524\\qquad\\mathrm{(B)}\\ 2015028\\qquad\\mathrm{(C)}\\ 2015033\\qquad\\mathrm{(D)}\\ 2016532\\qquad\\mathrm{(E)}\\ 2017033$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"617\" height=\"20\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/9/299b06b28a37a6585f0bc0e294786526d2c6dd7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F(2)=3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"70\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>By looking at various graphs, we obtain that, for most of the graphs\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/0/40002b02cef55c16f5abdbe2af1fe7d5db7238e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F(n) = n + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"104\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Notice that the solutions are basically reflections across <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/4/d04f02535ba0d4d2e88dd26b9ce36c7a24cd7057.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"48\" height=\"33\" />.\nHowever, when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/e/77eb0c927773ed1b909a0d12b086879d925df5be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"115\" height=\"18\" />, the middle apex of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sine\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Sine\">sine</a> curve touches the sine curve at the top only one time (instead of two reflected points), so we get here <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/c/7fc0f9ff12e6653f1b2127d8401e100e1ea17474.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F(n) = n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/4/414e0d13e3181f2ea337e5ac21344b35a6cea4d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3+4+5+5+7+8+9+9+\\cdots+2008$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"329\" height=\"14\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/a/cea565afe801cb75d9a04727f62641edcf029f60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= (1+2+3+4+5+\\cdots+2008) - 3 - 501$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"349\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/e/dfeb8aa8a290998263b28552a670691f4d36fd27.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\frac{(2008)(2009)}{2} - 504 = 2016532$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"260\" height=\"38\" />  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/b/bfbf4df7c25681bb178ddc50ee0c0d656aa25b5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathrm{(D)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/1/871a5070b1c511018316319158347e8bfae88996.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sin nx - \\sin x = 2\\left( \\cos \\frac {n + 1}{2}x\\right) \\left( \\sin \\frac {n - 1}{2}x\\right)\\]\" width=\"358\" height=\"43\" />\nSo <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/b/66b303a498a42967342e1075e8a2f92b7109f309.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin nx = \\sin x$\" width=\"106\" height=\"12\" /> if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/6/27624d981a9132c2bac6f6a3040af07897dc39e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos \\frac {n + 1}{2}x = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"116\" height=\"37\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/1/cd113c5d8f8099f296420a9b8639175524220d07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin \\frac {n - 1}{2}x = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"114\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p>The first occurs whenever <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/b/94b664ed5eb79f2edd40a3c500740552c55d6b83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {n + 1}{2}x = (j + 1/2)\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"163\" height=\"37\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/b/93b3920d0859dc9ef2ce948cb6b1b5680a2afb49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\frac {(2j + 1)\\pi}{n + 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"111\" height=\"40\" /> for some nonnegative integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/8122aa89ea6e80784c6513d22787ad86e36ad0cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"10\" height=\"17\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/5/9e563dadf9f47027587e05c789bc70e420342fa5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\leq \\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/c/83c8b2f5a58a86236c7606c9faf56a4e470c07bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j\\leq n/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"18\" />. So there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/7/917e848f321ab59a3251d414e25e183d5cea3916.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 + \\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"73\" height=\"19\" /> solutions in this case.\n</p><p>The second occurs whenever <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/e/43eb140768f5ca9c0f3f04cacdf9c6944a7fd0e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {n - 1}{2}x = k\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"101\" height=\"37\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/2/002bba2d90d7e94d5526726cbb7a15d452c503f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\frac {2k\\pi}{n - 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"79\" height=\"38\" /> for some nonnegative integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />. Here <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/7/6f7d4fd0ca7a05e4b1ce267b8bf1daad7bc1d3f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k\\leq \\frac {n - 1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"79\" height=\"37\" /> so that there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/b/fbb0a09b783fddf89df548af3204aa90f1a01e90.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left\\lfloor \\frac {n + 1}{2}\\right\\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"62\" height=\"43\" /> solutions here.\n</p><p>However, we overcount intersections. These occur whenever\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/e/eced9caeb4854046455cc26885c31312bfde4ca2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac {2j + 1}{n + 1} = \\frac {2k}{n - 1}\\]\" width=\"120\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/d/afdc681460be8e130fe26710fb88978f356aa940.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[k = \\frac {(2j + 1)(n - 1)}{2(n + 1)}\\]\" width=\"155\" height=\"43\" />\nwhich is equivalent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/9/fe9ded691a73fa2d057b2dd3fe1369d751620aab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2(n + 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"64\" height=\"18\" /> dividing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/7/3e7d1dfb9bf5ddbe1ca55b64ccd367cda072be6c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2j + 1)(n - 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"118\" height=\"18\" />. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is even, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/7/3e7d1dfb9bf5ddbe1ca55b64ccd367cda072be6c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2j + 1)(n - 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"118\" height=\"18\" /> is odd, so this never happens. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/7/7c7e7ab44ee8a196de9a4c267313e6d47e616a65.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\equiv 3\\pmod{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"115\" height=\"18\" />, then there won't be intersections either, since a multiple of 8 can't divide a number which is not even a multiple of 4.\n</p><p>This leaves <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/6/3b60316060abced4173a0697d5c6509a3c085122.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\equiv 1\\pmod{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"115\" height=\"18\" />. In this case, the divisibility becomes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/4/bc4be239d67316fd329216074256a25e37b77c8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {n + 1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"44\" height=\"37\" /> dividing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/d/68d05bef44b94802b6a7c80030cb06b1e0c509b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2j + 1)\\frac {n - 1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"107\" height=\"37\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/4/bc4be239d67316fd329216074256a25e37b77c8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {n + 1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"44\" height=\"37\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/c/85c29bad8d95dc4bbfec75e4ec0ef43c7acac3ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {n - 1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"44\" height=\"37\" /> are relatively prime (subtracting twice the second number from the first gives 1), <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/4/bc4be239d67316fd329216074256a25e37b77c8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {n + 1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"44\" height=\"37\" /> must divide <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/c/18c7c6ab28cd83033ec7e0b4a8311dd1c537aa70.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2j + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"48\" height=\"16\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/0/af017b12bbb62622622736e3dd6430ca0ab37dc9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j\\leq \\frac {n - 1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"81\" height=\"38\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/c/e1c10f1d2769bc8514c550080a97aec655d16dad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2j + 1\\leq n &lt; 2\\cdot \\frac {n + 1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"175\" height=\"37\" />. Then there is only one intersection, namely when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/b/90b0e6cc42275e3cfe1e445cfbbcaf4268072f9e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j = \\frac {n - 1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"78\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p>Therefore we find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/6/2663041fd2b9fbd85c85ad832f67c7a743a09c52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" /> is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/8/4e8cf2150b1aefa2f1b3cf4dd552b905b9df4a38.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 + \\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor + \\left \\lfloor \\frac {n + 1}{2}\\right\\rfloor = n + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"231\" height=\"43\" />, unless <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/6/3b60316060abced4173a0697d5c6509a3c085122.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\equiv 1\\pmod{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"115\" height=\"18\" />, in which case it is one less, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. The problem may then be finished as in Solution 1.\n</p><p>Note from Williamgolly:\nAn easier way to see that there is an extra point of intersection at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/e/77eb0c927773ed1b909a0d12b086879d925df5be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"115\" height=\"18\" /> is that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/5/055febd839bf14ac6a2bdef8bde744c2c1783896.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_2(2j+1)=0,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"120\" height=\"20\" /> so we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/3/173ea34002bfeecc5c7a46b7a0a17d5060812a52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_2(n-1)&gt;v_2(n+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"168\" height=\"18\" /> since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/2/ba25db73b49f24af50919bd482d7307e684e5c28.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_2(2k) \\geq 1 &gt;v_2(2j+1).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"190\" height=\"18\" />  Therefore, we suspect that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/e/77eb0c927773ed1b909a0d12b086879d925df5be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"115\" height=\"18\" /> has an extra point of intersection.  Testing, we see this is true.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\